      en: "Drenthe, located in the northeastern part of the Netherlands, is often called the 'cycling province' and is celebrated for its rugged natural landscapes and prehistoric heritage. Unlike much of the reclaimed land in the Netherlands, Drenthe is largely a sandy plateau, featuring ancient heaths, peat bogs, and forests. Its most famous landmarks are the 54 'hunebedden'—megalithic tomb structures built by the Funnelbeaker culture around 3000 BC using massive glacial erratics. These sites make Drenthe the archaeological capital of the country. The provincial capital, Assen, is internationally famous for the TT Circuit, which hosts the Dutch leg of the MotoGP world championship. Drenthe's economy has transitioned from peat extraction and traditional farming to a focus on green energy, logistics, and high-quality tourism. The province is home to three national parks, including the Drents-Friese Wold and Dwingelderveld, providing vast spaces for hiking and bird watching. Small 'esdorpen' (village greens) like Orvelte offer a glimpse into historical rural life with their preserved thatched-roof farmhouses. Culturally, Drenthe holds a quiet, stoic charm, reflected in the Drents Museum in Assen, which houses extraordinary bog bodies and archaeological finds. The province also bears the somber legacy of Camp Westerbork, a transit camp during the Holocaust, now a site of education and remembrance. For travelers, Drenthe offers a profound sense of space and tranquility, with over 1,400 kilometers of cycle paths winding through some of the most serene environments in the Low Countries.",
